Make the keychain union crypto/x509's system root store on darwin by serving it
from loadSystemRoots, instead of claiming the fallback-roots mechanism at init.
A program's own SetFallbackRoots call (e.g. importing
golang.org/x/crypto/x509roots/fallback) then behaves as upstream -- ignored
unless GODEBUG=x509usefallbackroots=1 -- instead of panicking, and the keychain
is only enumerated when a verification first needs the roots.
GODEBUG=x509usefallbackroots=0 returns upstream's systemPool marker, selecting
Apple's verifier. With GODEBUG=x509usefallbackroots=0 the union is not built,
so the keychain-distrust veto does not apply to Go-verifier runs with custom
Roots (upstream's behavior); with =1 it still applies. loadSystemRoots has had
this shape since Go 1.18.

It also makes upstream's systemVerify safe on older macOS: the SSL policy is
passed as a single SecPolicyRef rather than inside a one-element CFArray
(on OS X 10.9, SecTrustEvaluate faults when the policies argument is an array
built by CFArrayCreateMutable, which has no retain callbacks), and below 10.15
the evaluation and chain use SecTrustEvaluate and SecTrustGetCertificateAtIndex,
the chain loop adapted from Go 1.24's
(https://github.com/golang/go/blob/go1.24.13/src/crypto/x509/root_darwin.go).
10.15+ keeps upstream's calls.

